<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - HW10, online version: Stokes' theorem and the divergence      -->
<!-- theorem.  Auto-graded and randomized: each problem draws                 -->
<!-- its constants in <setup> and the answer objects are built from them, so  -->
<!-- every student sees the same problem with different numbers.  Every blank -->
<!-- is graded on its own, so partial credit survives.                        -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Notation follows the text: fluxes are written                           -->
<!--   \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma,                   -->
<!-- circulation is \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}, and the boundary    -->
<!-- curve is called C.                                                       -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Each <solution> is a bare element whose *children* carry                 -->
<!-- component="hw10-solutions"; the element itself is never dropped and it   -->
<!-- carries include-automatic="no".  See the comment at the top of           -->
<!-- source/review1-practice.ptx for why that matters.                        -->
<!-- Its three Stokes' theorem problems (ex-hw10o-stokes-hemisphere,        -->
<!-- ex-hw10o-verify-hemisphere, ex-hw10o-verify-cone) moved to Assignment 9  -->
<!-- (hw9-online.ptx).  "One Field, Three Theorems" stays here, since it     -->
<!-- needs the divergence theorem as well.                                    -->
<!-- Three divergence-theorem problems (ex-r4p-verify-cylinder,           -->
<!-- ex-r4p-tetrahedron, ex-r4p-cone) moved here from Review Problems #4    -->
<!-- under their old xml:ids, placed after Flux Out of a Box; their         -->
<!-- solutions were rewritten in this file's pattern (bare                  -->
<!-- <solution include-automatic="no">, children carrying                   -->
<!-- component="hw10-solutions").                                           -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-hw10-online" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 10</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="section-stokes-theorem" text="title"/> and
      <xref ref="section-divergence-theorem" text="title"/>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Numbers.</term> Give numbers as decimals to at least three
          places, with no units and no <m>\pi</m>. A negative answer needs
          its minus sign.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Expressions.</term> Type only the expression itself. Write
          products with <c>*</c> and powers with <c>^</c>, and write
          functions with parentheses: <c>cos(u)</c>, <c>sqrt(x^2+y^2)</c>,
          <c>e^z</c>. So the expression <m>2x + e^z</m> is typed
          <c>2*x + e^z</c>. Spaces are ignored.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-hw10-online">

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-divergence-box">
    <title>Flux Out of a Box</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="x0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="-3" max="0" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="w" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="z1" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="3" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="k" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="x1" context="number">
        <de-expression>x0+w</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="cospiece" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="val" context="number">
        <de-expression>(3/2)*w*(z1^2-z0^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
        d\sigma</m>, where
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \left\langle \sin(\pi x),\ zy^3,\
        z^2 + <eval obj="k"/>x \right\rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>S</m> is the surface of the box
        <m><eval obj="x0"/> \le x \le <eval obj="x1"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le 1</m>,
        <m><eval obj="z0"/> \le z \le <eval obj="z1"/></m>, with
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> pointing outward.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <m>\pi\cos(\pi x)</m> term of <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}</m>
        contributes
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="cospiece" width="12"/>
        to the triple integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        The flux is
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="val" width="16"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="cospiece.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(cospiece.value({})))" max="cospiece.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(cospiece.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="val.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))" max="val.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(val.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        The box is closed, so the divergence theorem turns six surface
        integrals into one triple integral. Here
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = \pi\cos(\pi x) + 3y^2z + 2z .
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        The first term integrates to
        <m>\left[\sin(\pi x)\right]_{x_0}^{x_1} = 0</m>, because both
        endpoints are integers and <m>\sin</m> vanishes at every integer
        multiple of <m>\pi</m>. That is why it contributes nothing at all.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        For the rest, with <m>w = x_1 - x_0</m> and
        <m>\int_0^1 3y^2\, dy = 1</m>,
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = w\int_{z_0}^{z_1}\!\!\int_0^1 \left(3y^2z + 2z\right) dy\, dz
        = w\int_{z_0}^{z_1} 3z\, dz
        = \frac{3w}{2}\left(z_1^2 - z_0^2\right).
      </me>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 6.  Verify the Divergence Theorem on a cylinder  (moved from Review Problems #4, #6)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-verify-cylinder">
    <title>Verifying the Divergence Theorem on a Cylinder</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="h" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="both" context="number">
        <de-expression>A*h*pi</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Verify the Divergence Theorem for the vector field and region:
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathbf{F} = \langle <eval obj="A"/>x,\ <eval obj="B"/>z,\
        <eval obj="C"/>y \rangle
      </me>
      <p>
        and the region <m>x^2 + y^2 \le 1</m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le <eval obj="h"/></m>. Give each side as a decimal
        number.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{S} \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="both" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iiint_{W} \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\ dV =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="both" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="both.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))" max="both.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="both.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))" max="both.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(both.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        With <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle Ax, Bz, Cy\rangle</m> we get
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = A</m>, so
        <m>\iiint_W \nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}\,dV
        = A\,\text{Vol}(W) = A\pi h</m>. Computing the surface integral
        directly: on the side, <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}
        = A\cos^2\theta + Bz\sin\theta</m> integrates to <m>A h\pi</m>; the
        top and bottom disks contribute <m>0</m> by symmetry, since there
        <m>\mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \pm Cy</m>. Both sides equal
        <m>A h \pi</m>, verifying the theorem.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Here <m>A = 3</m> and <m>h = 2</m>, so both sides equal
<m>3(2)\pi = 6\pi \approx 18.85</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 11.  Divergence theorem on a tetrahedron  (moved from Review Problems #4, #11)                  -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-tetrahedron">
    <title>Flux Through the Surface of a Tetrahedron</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1+x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c*(a+4)/24</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="vol" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c/6</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the Divergence Theorem to calculate the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F}</m> across <m>S</m>, where
        <m>\mathbf{F} = z\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} +
        zx\,\mathbf{k}</m> and <m>S</m> is the surface of the tetrahedron
        enclosed by the coordinate planes and the plane
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{x}{<eval obj="a"/>} + \frac{y}{<eval obj="b"/>}
        + \frac{z}{<eval obj="c"/>} = 1.
      </me>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="10"/>
        (an expression in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp object="vol" min="vol.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(vol.value({})))" max="vol.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(vol.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 0 + 1 + x = 1 + x</m>. The
        tetrahedron <m>E</m> has vertices <m>(0,0,0)</m>,
        <m>(a,0,0)</m>, <m>(0,b,0)</m>, <m>(0,0,c)</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S \mathbf{F}\cdot\hat{\mathbf{n}}\,d\sigma
        = \iiint_E (1+x)\,dV
        = \frac{bc}{2}\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\frac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \frac{abc\,(a+4)}{24},
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        using <m>\int_0^{a}(1+x)\left(1-\tfrac{x}{a}\right)^2 dx
        = \tfrac{a}{3} + \tfrac{a^2}{12} = \tfrac{a(a+4)}{12}</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Here <m>a = 3</m>, <m>b = 2</m> and <m>c = 4</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{(3)(2)(4)(3+4)}{24} = 7</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 10.  Flux out of a closed cone  (moved from Review Problems #4, #10)                            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r4p-cone">
    <title>Flux Out of a Closed Cone</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="divF" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*a*y</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="flux" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*r^4*pi/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the flux of
        <m>\mathbf{F} = <eval obj="B"/>z\,\mathbf{i}
        + <eval obj="a"/>y^2\,\mathbf{j} + <eval obj="C"/>x\,\mathbf{k}</m>
        out of the closed cone <m>y = \sqrt{x^2 + z^2}</m>, with
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="r"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="divF" width="10"/>
        (an expression in <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, <m>z</m>)
      </p>
      <p>
        flux <m>=</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="flux" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="flux.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))" max="flux.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(flux.value({})))"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2ay</m>, where <m>a</m> is the
        coefficient of <m>y^2</m>. By the Divergence Theorem, using
        cylindrical coordinates around the <m>y</m>-axis (the cone is
        <m>0 \le \rho \le y \le r</m>),
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \text{flux} = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{r}\!\!\int_0^{y}
        2ay\,\rho\,d\rho\,dy\,d\theta
        = 2\pi\int_0^{r} a y^3\,dy = \frac{a r^4 \pi}{2}.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Here <m>a = 3</m> and <m>r = 2</m>, so the flux is
<m>\dfrac{3\left(2^4\right)\pi}{2} = 24\pi \approx 75.40</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw10o-three-theorems">
    <title>One Field, Three Theorems</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="valA" context="number">
        <de-expression>3*pi/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="valC" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi/3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="valZ" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let
        <m>\mathbf{F} = -y^3\,\mathbf{i} + x^3\,\mathbf{j}
        + z^2\,\mathbf{k}</m>,
        let <m>S</m> be the paraboloid <m>z = 1 - x^2 - y^2</m> with
        <m>z \ge 0</m>, oriented upward, and let <m>C</m> be its boundary
        curve<mdash/>the unit circle in the <m>xy</m>-plane, traversed
        counterclockwise as seen from above. Let <m>D</m> be the unit disk
        in the plane <m>z = 0</m>, and let <m>E</m> be the solid region
        enclosed between <m>S</m> and <m>D</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (a) Explain why the space curve integral
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> reduces
        exactly to a plane integral, and evaluate it using Green's theorem,
        <xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/>. Its value is
        <fillin name="b1" mode="number" ansobj="valA" width="16"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (b) Verify your answer using Stokes' theorem,
        <xref ref="thm-stokes"/>, with the surface <m>S</m>: the flux
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m> equals
        <fillin name="b2" mode="number" ansobj="valA" width="16"/>.
        Then explain, without computing, why any surface having <m>C</m>
        as its boundary would give the same flux of
        <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (c) Use the divergence theorem, <xref ref="thm-divergence"/>, to
        compute the outward flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> itself through the
        closed surface <m>S \cup D</m>:
        <fillin name="b3" mode="number" ansobj="valC" width="16"/>.
        The flux through the disk <m>D</m> alone is
        <fillin name="b4" mode="number" ansobj="valZ" width="10"/>,
        so the flux through the paraboloid alone is
        <fillin name="b5" mode="number" ansobj="valC" width="16"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        (d) In (b) you were allowed to swap surfaces freely; in (c) you
        were not<mdash/>the flux of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> through <m>S</m> and
        through <m>D</m> differ. Reconcile these two facts using the
        identity <m>\nabla \cdot (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) =</m>
        <fillin name="b6" mode="number" ansobj="valZ" width="10"/>
        together with the divergence theorem.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="valA.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valA.value({})))" max="valA.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valA.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="valA.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valA.value({})))" max="valA.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valA.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="valC.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valC.value({})))" max="valC.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valC.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="valZ.value({})-0.01" max="valZ.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="valC.value({})-Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valC.value({})))" max="valC.value({})+Math.max(0.01,1e-4*Math.abs(valC.value({})))"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <numcmp use-answer="yes" min="valZ.value({})-0.01" max="valZ.value({})+0.01"/>
          <feedback><p>Correct.</p></feedback>
        </test>
        <test><numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/><feedback><p>Incorrect.</p></feedback></test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
    <solution include-automatic="no">
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        (a) On the curve <m>C</m> we have <m>z \equiv 0</m>, so the third
        component of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> contributes <m>z^2\, dz = 0</m>
        along <m>C</m>. The line integral is therefore genuinely planar:
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \oint_C -y^3\, dx + x^3\, dy.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        By Green's theorem, <xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/>, with
        <m>M = -y^3</m> and <m>N = x^3</m>,
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \oint_C -y^3\, dx + x^3\, dy
        = \iint_D \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
        - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right) dA
        = \iint_D 3\left(x^2 + y^2\right) dA.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        In polar coordinates,
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_D 3\left(x^2 + y^2\right) dA
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1 3r^2 \cdot r\, dr\, d\theta
        = 2\pi \cdot \frac{3}{4} = \frac{3\pi}{2} \approx 4.712.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        (b) The curl is
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \nabla \times \mathbf{F} =
        \begin{vmatrix}
        \mathbf{i} \amp \mathbf{j} \amp \mathbf{k} \\
        \dfrac{\partial}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}
        \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
        -y^3 \amp x^3 \amp z^2
        \end{vmatrix}
        = \left(3x^2 + 3y^2\right) \mathbf{k}.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Stokes' theorem asserts
        <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
        = \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m>. Since <m>C</m> also bounds the
        flat disk <m>D</m> (with upward normal
        <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{k}</m>), we may evaluate the flux
        there:
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_D \left(3x^2 + 3y^2\right) \mathbf{k} \cdot \mathbf{k}\, dA
        = \iint_D 3\left(x^2 + y^2\right) dA = \frac{3\pi}{2},
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        in agreement with (a). As for surface independence: Stokes'
        theorem equates the flux of <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m>
        through <em>any</em> oriented surface with boundary <m>C</m> to
        the single number
        <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m>. The boundary
        circulation pins down the answer; the interior of the surface is
        irrelevant<mdash/>this is the point of
        <xref ref="sec-stokes-surface-independence" text="title"/>. Hence
        every surface spanning <m>C</m> carries curl-flux
        <m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m>.
      </p>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        (c) The divergence is
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F}
        = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(-y^3\right)
        + \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(x^3\right)
        + \frac{\partial}{\partial z}\left(z^2\right) = 2z</m>.
        By the divergence theorem, <xref ref="thm-divergence"/>, the
        outward flux through the closed surface <m>S \cup D</m> is
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_{S \cup D} \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \iiint_E 2z\, dV.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        Using cylindrical coordinates with <m>0 \le z \le 1 - r^2</m>:
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iiint_E 2z\, dV
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{1-r^2} 2z\, r\, dz\, dr\,
        d\theta
        = 2\pi \int_0^1 \left(1 - r^2\right)^2 r\, dr
        = 2\pi \cdot \frac{1}{6} = \frac{\pi}{3} \approx 1.047.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        On the bottom disk <m>D</m> the outward normal is
        <m>-\mathbf{k}</m> and
        <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot (-\mathbf{k}) = -z^2 = 0</m> since
        <m>z = 0</m> there. The bottom contributes nothing, so the
        paraboloid alone carries the full flux:
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \frac{\pi}{3}.
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        (d) Suppose <m>S_1</m> and <m>S_2</m> are two surfaces sharing
        the boundary <m>C</m>, oriented consistently. Reversing the
        orientation of <m>S_2</m> and gluing gives a closed surface
        enclosing a solid region <m>W</m>. For the curl field, the
        divergence theorem gives
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_{S_1} (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        - \iint_{S_2} (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
        \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \iiint_W \nabla \cdot (\nabla \times \mathbf{F})\, dV = 0,
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        because <m>\nabla \cdot (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) = 0</m>
        identically. Surface independence in (b) is thus equivalent to
        the vanishing divergence of a curl. The field <m>\mathbf{F}</m>
        itself enjoys no such privilege:
        <m>\nabla \cdot \mathbf{F} = 2z \not\equiv 0</m>, so
      </p>
      <me component="hw10-solutions">
        \iint_S \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        - \iint_D \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
        = \iiint_E 2z\, dV = \frac{\pi}{3} \ne 0,
      </me>
      <p component="hw10-solutions">
        and indeed we found <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m> through the paraboloid
        versus <m>0</m> through the disk. The moral: circulation-type
        quantities (curl-flux) depend only on the boundary curve, while
        flux-type quantities depend on the surface itself<mdash/>precisely
        because curls are divergence-free and general fields are not.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
